ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain: faces joined on a surface that closes on
itself without being periodic are bounded and trimmed on the periodic
B-spline made of that surface, not on the surface itself; a B-spline
surface is made periodic on a copy (mitcad#2623, follow-up of mitcad#2538).

When the merge joins B-spline faces (ConcatBSplines, as the booleans'
SimplifyResult asks for) and the faces meet at an edge with two pcurves on
their surface where it closes on itself smoothly, IntUnifyFaces makes the
surface periodic in that direction: a B-spline in place, any other surface
(an offset surface, say) approximated by a B-spline first. The relocation
to a new origin that follows, when the faces' union falls into two
intervals of the new period, took the bounds from the surface the faces
started from and trimmed that surface from the new origin over one
period. On a surface that was approximated the trim reached past the
original surface's bounds, and Geom_RectangularTrimmedSurface threw
("V parameters out of range"; a tab joined into a shelled bowl, whose
rounding and the rib ends on it lie on one such surface); the boolean's
caller then kept the faces unmerged. The relocation now bounds and trims
the surface the merged face is built on (the periodic B-spline when one
was made). A surface that already was a B-spline was made periodic in
place, so the faces' surface, shared with the input shape and with the
faces that were not merged, changed under them; it is copied first.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2623.

